and apart from  this  there's not much room for creativity or  let's face it career advancement which  is why a job at hondy grill advertised  with permanent full-time status little  experience  and a more than modest salary surprised  people because of its title line that's  why i guess it's very newsworthy because  no that's certainly not the case that  someone's going to be paid 25 an hour to  wash dishes but i think it reflects just  how  desperate our industry is right now to  attract labor according to a recent  restaurants canada survey of its members  nine out of ten canadian restaurants say  they could use more staff  admittedly employers have come to accept  that paying workers minimum wage in this  industry may be a thing of the past but  the salary offered for the dishwasher  position at the hondy grill is still  raising eyebrows if you're a fifty  thousand dollar as a dishwasher that's  the entry job what are you gonna pay  your second cook what are you going to  be your first cook chef to party sous  chef and keep going up until the chef  that mean that i'm charging you 95 to  100 for a plate of salmon it's  impossible to sustain now i had a brief  conversation with the owner and perhaps  she's tired of the publicity and didn't  want to do an interview but did say that  the job has been filled  and that ad no longer exists  and matagahi global news  still ahead what's behind the big c bump  in humpback whales  they're back sheep bells rang out in  spain's capital where locals welcomed an  annual tradition it allows shepherds to  guide their livestock south for the  winter using an ancient migration route  through downtown madrid the yearly event  started in 1994 but was canceled last  year because of the pandemic  a mystery is unfolding off the west  coast but it's one researchers are  welcoming the number of humpback whale  calves spotted near british columbia and  washington state this year has nearly  doubled that of last year kylie statton  has more on the baby boom and what may  be driving it  they're everywhere  incredible it's being called the  humpback comeback  the number of whales making their way to  the salish sea on the rise for the past  two decades but this year a new record  just the sheer number of babies that  we're seeing in 2021 is something that i  certainly was not expecting going into  this season according to the center for  whale research 21 calves have been  photographed and documented throughout  inland washington and british columbia  that's the highest annual number on  record for the region surpassing 2020  when 11 calves were born so we're almost  double what we had last year and last  year was a really great year as well it  all started with big mama the first  whale documented back in 1997.  since then the species has been  migrating each summer to feed in the  nutrient-rich waters off bc's coast  slowly rebuilding the population that  really seems to be what it is is as  humpbacks rediscover the area others are  following them and there seems to be  enough food here to entice them to make  it a regular part of their migration  fall is peak of humpback whale activity  in the salish sea their last feeding  opportunity before swimming roughly 5  000 kilometers to their breeding grounds  near hawaii mexico and central america  where they mate and give birth before  making their return  and if there's a calf in toe naturalists  breathe a collective sigh of relief  because you never know right if both mom  and calf are going to make it back there  are several theories behind the  resurgence that is the million dollar  question it's possibly linked to an  abundance of food for the whales or it  could be as simple as the growing adult  whale population producing more calves  but one thing is certain their presence  creates a hazard for those on the water  and boaters need to not only be aware  but also keep their distance legally it  is 100 meters but 200 meters can keeps  things much simpler as well after all  the better protected they are the better  researchers can track the trend kylie  stanton global news  up next the naked truth about earth from  the lens of an artist  here's one more accolade for canadian  olympian maggie mcneil she was named  best female athlete at the tokyo  olympics by the association of national  olympic committees mcneil competed at  her first olympic games this past summer  and was it ever a triumph she won gold  in the 100 meter butterfly  silver in freestyle relay and bronze in  the medley relay  well done in one week the u.n climate  conference known as cop26 will get  underway in glasgow scotland the goal of  this summit is to deliver a new  agreement to significantly limit  greenhouse gas emissions and stave off  the worst effects of human-caused  climate change while world leaders are  wrangling over targets artists are doing  their part to open the world's eyes to  the reality of our environment as mike  truly explains one photographer is  laying those concerns bare with his  provocative masterpiece  on a rocky hillside overlooking the dead  sea american artist spencer tonic took a  stand against climate change as only he  can  from the roof of a camper he  photographed 200 nude models all painted  stark white  i'm going to be  using  and working with the brave participants  to form  salt pillars  which reference  the  salt pillars formed by springs  in the dead sea  when fresh water enters the dead sea  which we need a lot of tonic first  visited the dead sea a decade ago for an  art installation involving a thousand  nude models five years after the fact  all that was left was crusty sand  because the dead sea which at 430 meters  below sea level is the lowest point on  earth is seeing its water recede by  roughly a meter every year in part  because israel and jordan have diverted  much of the water that feeds into the  dead sea for agriculture and human  consumption it's a really important  treasure here in the middle east and  it's going to disappear really quite  soon if we don't act now so it was very  important for us to come here and  participate in this  provocation of an art show  art as a tool to highlight climate  change has become popular in recent  years in florida panthers melting in the  sun eventually revealed a message more  heat less wildlife  and off the coast of scotland horizontal  lights installed on rocks and buildings  are meant to show the dangers of rising  sea levels  like tonic's nude installations the goal  is to shock and create something world  leaders can't ignore  microlight global news toronto and that  is global national for this sunday night  i'm robin gill tonight your canada is  the moon rising over the spirit sands  near brandon manitoba please keep  sending your photos to viewers at  globalnational.com  thank you for watching donna friesen  will be back at the anchor desk tomorrow  and until next weekend have a great week  [Music]  you
 

